  "completion": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to forecast the probability that by each of these deadlines (2026, 2030, etc.), at least one of the Millennium Prize Problems (excluding Poincar\u00e9) will have a correct solution where AI contributed a key new mathematical argument. Let me think through this step by step.\n\nFirst, understanding the problem. The Millennium Prize Problems are seven major unsolved math problems, each with a $1 million reward. As of the forecast date in 2025, one (Poincar\u00e9) has been solved, leaving five others. The user is excluding the Poincar\u00e9 problem, so we're dealing with five: Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer, Hodge, Navier-Stokes, P vs NP, Riemann, and Yang-Mills. The event in question is that a solution (not just partial progress) is publicly available and includes an AI-generated step. Also, the solution must meet the original Clay Institute's criteria.\n\nNow, I need to estimate probabilities for each deadline. The deadlines are 2026, 2030, 2035, 2040, 2050. The probabilities should be non-decreasing as time goes on, i.e., no earlier deadline has a higher probability than a later one. Also, the probabilities must be between 0 and 1.\n\nStarting with 2026. That's just a year after the forecast date (2025). Given how hard these problems are, it seems extremely unlikely that any full solution with significant AI input will be published in a year. Maybe some progress, but a complete solution? Probably not. So the 2026 probability should be very low, maybe 0.01 or something like that.\n\n2030 is five years out. By then, maybe AI math capabilities have advanced. But still, these problems are notoriously tough. For example, P vs NP might have AI tools explored particular cases, but a general proof? Unlikely. However, maybe Navier-Stokes or perhaps Riemann gets some attention. Let's say 2030 has maybe 5-10% probability.\n\n2035, ten years after forecast date. That's 10 more years from 2025. AI may have had more development in mathematical reasoning, with collaboration between mathematicians and AI systems. For example, AI could generate conjectures or check extensive cases, leading to a step in a solution. Maybe 2035's probability is around 15-20%.\n\n2040 is a quarter of a century from 2025. By then, AI systems could be much more integrated into mathematical research. Perhaps in proving a conjecture by exploring vast possibilities or finding patterns that humans couldn't. If AI develops to the point where it can autonomously assist in proving long-standing conjectures, maybe 2040 has 30-40% chance.\n\n2050 is 25 years from the forecast. That's a long time for AI to mature. If AI doesn't solve these problems by 2040, maybe by 2050 it's more certain, but given the difficulty, perhaps 50-60%? However, maybe some problems (like Riemann Hypothesis) are so deep that even with AI, they might not be solved by then. So maybe the probability isn't that high. Maybe 30-40% as an upper bound.\n\nBut I need to make sure the probabilities are non-decreasing. So if 2026 is 1%, 2030 10%, 2035 20%, 2040 40%, 2050 60%, that's possible. But maybe the increases are more linear. Also, for example, by 2050, maybe the probability is 50-60%, but I'm not sure. Given the difficulty, even after 25 years, maybe the chance is 30-40% is more realistic.\n\nAnother angle: the Clay problems are all very different. For example, P vs NP is a foundational problem in computer science. If AI can help in generating proofs, perhaps a more combinatorial problem like P vs NP could be approached with AI's help. But even so, a significant breakthrough seems unlikely in the next two decades.\n\nLooking at recent trends: AI has already helped in solving some problems in mathematics. For example, in 2023 there was a paper using AI to find a certain problem's solution in topology. The de Bruijn-Newman constant approach to Riemann Hypothesis has seen progress with computational methods. Maybe AI will assist in numerical approaches to some of these problems, but proving them requires analytical techniques. So for some problems, maybe computational progress is possible earlier, leading to steps in a solution by AI.\n\nConsidering collaboration: If AI is used to handle complex computations or explore cases, perhaps a human-AI team could make progress. For example, in the Navier-Stokes equations, AI might verify many scenarios or suggest new mathematical avenues.\n\nGiven all that, the probabilities might be as follows:\n\n2026: Very low. Let's say 0.5% (0.005)\n\n2030: 1-5%. Maybe 0.02 (2%)\n\n2035: 5-15%. Maybe 0.1 (10%)\n\n2040: 20% (0.2)\n\n2050: 30-40%. Maybe 0.35\n\nBut I need to make sure each subsequent probability is higher than the previous. Let me adjust:\n\nIf 2026 is 0.005, 2030 could be 0.02, 2035 0.1, 2040 0.2, 2050 0.4.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the increase is more gradual. For instance, 2026: 0.001 (0.1%), 2030: 0.005, 2035: 0.01, 2040: 0.05, 2050: 0.2.\n\nBut the user says to account for the possibility that the event never occurs. So the probabilities should approach but not exceed 1.0. However, since these are cumulative, each deadline has the cumulative chance up to that point.\n\nLooking at the original example: in the sample given, the answer used probabilities increasing from 0.01 to 0.3. So I need to pick numbers that are non-decreasing, perhaps with small increments. Maybe starting at 0.005 for 2026, then 0.01 for 2030, 0.02 for 2035, 0.05 for 2040, and 0.15 for 2050. That's non-decreasing and plausible.\n\nWait, but 0.005 (2026), then 0.01 (2030), 0.02 (2035), 0.05 (2040), and 0.15 (2050). That's a cumulative increase per deadline.\n\nIs there a basis for these numbers? Let's consider that solving a Millennium Problem with AI contribution is an extremely high bar. Even with AI, these require deep insights. The user says \"correct complete solution\", not just steps. So it's very hard. But AI could assist in certain aspects.\n\nThe higher probability by 2050 would be because over a longer timespan, there's more opportunity for AI-aided solutions. However, given the difficulty of the problems, even with AI, the probability of at least one may be 15-20% by 2050? Or maybe higher.\n\nAlternatively, look at how many of these problems have been solved or have partial progress. For example, the Riemann Hypothesis: some approaches suggest new regions where the zeta function has zeros, but full proof is lacking. If AI can generate new approaches or help in proving certain properties, maybe by 2050.\n\nNavier-Stokes: The question is about the existence of smooth solutions in three dimensions. If AI can simulate more complex scenarios and help in finding patterns or proving properties like regularity.\n\nGiven that, I think the probabilities are low in the near term but increase over time.\n\nI'll go with the following structure:\n\n\"2026\": 0.005,\n\n\"2030\": 0.01,\n\n\"2035\": 0.02,\n\n\"2040\": 0.05,\n\n\"2050\": 0.15\n\nThese values are low, but each subsequent year's deadline has a higher chance. They are all increasing and under 1. They represent the cumulative probability by each deadline of at least one problem being solved with an AI contribution.\n\nSo the final JSON would look like that.\n</think>\n\n{\n  \"2026\": 0.005,\n  \"2030\": 0.01,\n  \"2035\": 0.02,\n  \"2040\": 0.05,\n  \"2050\": 0.15\n}",
